---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image-to-100kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={100} />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why “100 KB exactly” is hard, and how this page does it</h2>
    <p>
      No formula turns a quality setting into a file size. Quality 74 can give 42 KB for a studio
      portrait and 380 KB for a photo of a hedge, because JPEG spends its bits on detail, and the
      amount of detail in those two images is very different. With a quality slider alone, you must
      guess, upload again, and guess again.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page measures. It encodes at quality 92 and checks the byte count. If that fits, it stops,
      and you keep the best quality. If not, it does a binary search: it encodes at the midpoint of the
      remaining range; if the result fits, it keeps it and raises the floor; if not, it lowers the
      ceiling. Nine or ten rounds narrow the range to one quality point, and you download the highest
      encode that fits. The result card lists each attempt with its quality and byte count.
    </p>

    <h2>The reason upload sites cannot do this</h2>
    <p>
      On a server-based compressor, each attempt is a round trip: your photo goes up, an encoder runs,
      and the result comes back. Ten attempts are ten uploads. For this reason those services offer
      one quality slider and one attempt, and their free tiers allow two or three jobs a day. Here the
      encoder is MozJPEG compiled to WebAssembly, on your own CPU. Ten encodes of a 12-megapixel photo
      take less than a second, so an exact target is practical.
    </p>

    <h2>What actually fits in 100 KB</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Content</th><th>Comfortable size at 100 KB</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Passport-style portrait, plain background</td><td>1200 × 1600 px at quality 80+</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Product shot on white</td><td>1600 px wide, visually lossless</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ordinary outdoor snapshot</td><td>1200 px wide at quality 70–80</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Foliage, crowds, water, film grain</td><td>800 px wide, and it still looks soft</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Screenshot with text</td><td>Use PNG and resize; JPEG adds rings around letters</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>If quality alone cannot get there</h2>
    <p>
      When even quality 5 is too large (usual for very large or very detailed photos), the tool starts
      a second binary search on the pixel dimensions, with a Lanczos3 resample. It reports the scale it
      used. If the dimensions must not change, turn that fallback off. You then get the smallest file
      the quality search can make, with a warning that the target was missed.
    </p>

    <h2>Get more out of the same 100 KB</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Crop first. The encoder spends no bits on pixels you remove. A tight crop at 100 KB always looks better than a wide scene at 100 KB.</li>
      <li>Limit the width with the width field. A photo shown at 800 px does not need 4000 px of data.</li>
      <li>Try WebP. Where the destination accepts it, WebP keeps about 25–35% more detail than JPEG at the same byte count.</li>
      <li>Start from the original. Compressing an already-compressed JPEG adds to the errors of the first generation. Always use the camera file.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Kilobytes, kibibytes and the 2% margin</h2>
    <p>
      The tool counts 1 KB as 1024 bytes, as the file browser of your operating system does, and aims
      2% under the limit. Upload validators compare against a hard byte limit and reject a file one byte
      over, and some measure the base64-encoded size, not the raw size. A 100 KB target thus gives a
      file of about 100,000 bytes or less, which satisfies both the 1024 and the 1000 interpretation.
    </p>

    <h2>Batch and ZIP</h2>
    <p>
      Drop in as many images as you like. Each one gets its own search to the same target, with a
      progress row, and you can download them one by one or as one ZIP. There is no file cap, daily
      quota or account, because there is no server cost.
    </p>

    <h2>Other targets</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-50kb">50 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb">200 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-500kb">500 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb">1 MB</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>. For a quality slider, use
      <a href="/compress-image">Compress Image</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
